# AXaxmlIDE feature roadmap

## Goal

Build a lightweight, approachable IDE for Avalonia and C# projects. Keep the
familiar Visual Studio feel while borrowing useful workflow ideas from Visual
Studio, JetBrains Rider, and VS Code with C# Dev Kit. This is a staged roadmap,
not a promise to reproduce every feature of those products.

Use the checklist to coordinate work between an agent and the project owner:
check items off only when implemented and verified. Keep debugger and other
large features behind the workspace, editor, and language-service foundations
they depend on.

Settings-window rule: whenever a new option is deemed user-configurable, add
it to the appropriate section of the Settings window as part of that feature,
and persist it through the settings model where appropriate. Do not add
one-off preference controls to the main toolbar.

## Architecture direction

- Do not add a DI container by default. Keep application composition explicit
  until multiple windows, service lifetimes, or test seams create a concrete
  need for one.
- Put application actions and state in view models, expose user commands with
  CommunityToolkit.Mvvm, and bind XAML controls to those commands.
- For new features, prefer commands and bindings over adding code-behind event
  handlers; use code-behind only where the behavior needs direct control/UI
  coordination.
- Keep control-specific event handling, dialogs, editor rendering, pointer
  placement, and flyout behavior in views. Do not force those interactions into
  view models just to eliminate event handlers.
- Extract cohesive, reusable workflows into focused classes under `Services/`
  and call them from view models. Prefer feature-oriented types over splitting
  large files into partial classes without changing their responsibilities.

## Local project dependencies

The solution keeps its MSBuild host and XAML completion engine projects under
`Projects/`, so they can be opened and edited alongside the IDE without relying
on sibling checkouts. The completion engine is a focused copy of the engine
sources used by the Avalonia Visual Studio extension; its namespaces are under
`AXaxmlIDE.XamlCompletion`, and its projects target .NET 10. Keep its MIT
license in `Projects/CompletionEngine/LICENSE.md` when modifying or distributing
those sources.

## Current baseline

The codebase already includes:

- [x] Open folders, `.sln` and `.slnx` solutions; show project files in a
  solution tree with a full-row hit target and double-click-to-open behavior.
- [x] Open, edit, save, and close documents in tabs; track unsaved edits and
  prompt before closing a solution.
- [x] Build and run a project, with build and console output panes.
- [x] Show language-server diagnostics in the editor and Problems pane.
- [x] Provide completion, hover information, and signature help for supported
  C# and AXAML contexts.
- [x] Preview Avalonia AXAML while editing.
- [x] Select a TextMate editor theme/font, adjust font size with Ctrl+mouse
  wheel or the toolbar, and toggle line numbers/current-line highlighting.
- [x] Store breakpoint locations on open documents; this is not yet a debugger.
- [x] Persist whether Solution Explorer is docked on the left or right in the
  user settings file.

## Roadmap

### 0. Stabilize workspace and settings foundations

Do these first: later navigation, layout, and debugging features need reliable
workspace state and predictable settings.

- [x] Add a typed settings model and persistent storage for editor appearance:
  theme, font family and size, line numbers, and current-line highlighting.
- [x] Load settings at startup and apply changes immediately; changes are saved
  to the user's application-data `AXaxmlIDE/settings.json`.
- [x] Define user-wide versus workspace-specific settings and their precedence.
  Phase 0 implements user-wide settings; if workspace settings are introduced,
  `.axaxmlide/settings.json` will override user defaults.
- [x] Handle missing, invalid, or outdated settings explicitly and safely.
  Missing files use defaults; malformed/unsupported files and invalid values
  produce a status-bar warning and fall back to defaults or the affected field.
  Settings schema version 1 migrates to the current schema with a default
  explorer side; older schemas default format-on-save to off.
- [ ] Persist useful session state such as the last workspace and open documents
  only after document recovery and unsaved-change behavior are defined.
- [x] Add tests for settings defaults, load/save round trips, and invalid files.

### 1. Make everyday navigation and search efficient

- [x] Add solution-wide literal text search with result locations and navigation.
  Search includes unsaved text in open documents, skips common build/dependency
  folders and binary files, and reports unreadable and oversized files.
- [x] Add a deliberate replace workflow to file/content search. Replace All
  prepares a plan, asks for confirmation, treats replacement text literally,
  leaves open documents dirty for review/save, writes closed files while
  retaining detected encoding, and refuses plans over 5,000 matches. Files
  changed after preview are not overwritten.
- [x] Add Quick Open by file name (Ctrl+P) and workspace symbol search (Ctrl+T).
  Results are capped to keep the picker responsive and exclude common generated
  and dependency folders.
- [x] Add Go to Definition (F12), Find All References (Shift+F12), and Rename
  Symbol (Ctrl+R) where the active language server advertises support. Multiple
  navigation results can be selected; rename changes are confirmed and opened
  unsaved for review.
- [x] Add Solution Explorer context actions to open files, copy paths, refresh
  folders/projects/solutions, and build or run projects.
- [x] Add a Find in Files menu command and Ctrl+Shift+F shortcut.
- [x] Add menu/keyboard entry points for Find in Files, Quick Open, workspace
  symbols, Go to Definition, Find All References, and Rename Symbol. Commands
  are capability-checked and report when the active server doesn't support
  them.

### 2. Complete the editing and language-service workflow

- [x] Request LSP code actions for the caret/selection and diagnostics; show
  quick fixes and refactorings through Ctrl+Period, the editor context menu,
  and a wider gutter lightbulb hit target. Both lightbulbs open the same compact
  flyout of 10-point actions without descriptions. Hovering a diagnostic with
  available actions opens that flyout at the pointer with the diagnostic message
  as its header, instead of a competing tooltip. Unresolved actions are resolved
  before application, original diagnostic metadata is sent to the server, text
  edits update the editor and remain unsaved for review, legacy and modern
  server commands are supported, and server commands can apply edits through
  LSP workspace/applyEdit.
- [ ] Unify completion suggestions with diagnostics and code actions so related
  fixes/refactorings are discoverable from completion as well as diagnostic
  hover.
- [x] Add an explicit Format Document command where supported by the language
  server; formatting changes remain unsaved for review.
- [x] Add format-on-save where supported, controlled by the persisted
  "Format documents on save when supported" editor setting. Older settings
  schemas migrate with the option disabled. Formatting edits are applied to
  the open document before saving.
- [ ] Improve completion UX with snippets, filtering, commit characters, and
  consistent C# / AXAML behavior.
- [x] Add navigation between Problems and source locations, including
  next/previous diagnostic commands (F8 / Shift+F8).
- [ ] Surface unsupported or unavailable language-server capabilities instead
  of presenting nonfunctional commands.
- [x] Add AXAML Go to Definition for resolved metadata types by opening
  decompiled, read-only source; show a status-bar message when a type cannot be
  resolved or decompiled. ILSpy powers the decompilation; unresolved and
  unsupported cases report a clear status message.

### 3. Improve editor layout and tab workflows

- [ ] Support multiple editor groups/split views with independent active tabs.
- [ ] Allow dragging a tab into a separate window, and dragging it back.
- [ ] Preserve document identity, edits, diagnostics, and caret/scroll position
  when moving a tab between groups or windows.
- [ ] Save and restore editor layout and tab state using the settings/session
  foundations above.
- [ ] Add tab navigation, pinning, and predictable close behavior for groups of
  tabs.

### 4. Build a debugger on top of the project and breakpoint foundations

This is intentionally later work. First settle project selection, run
configuration, process lifecycle, and breakpoint persistence.

- [ ] Define launch profiles and startup-project selection, including support
  for applicable `launchSettings.json` profiles.
- [ ] Introduce a debugger adapter/protocol boundary rather than coupling debug
  UI directly to process launching. Initial direction: use the Debug Adapter
  Protocol (DAP) to communicate with an external adapter.
  [NetCoreDbg](https://github.com/Samsung/netcoredbg) is the initial
  MIT-licensed backend and is installed separately as a standalone executable.
  No maintained MIT NuGet DAP client was identified, so keep the client
  transport narrow and in-app rather than adding an adapter/server-side
  protocol package such as
  [Microsoft.VisualStudio.Shared.VSCodeDebugProtocol](https://www.nuget.org/packages/Microsoft.VisualStudio.Shared.VSCodeDebugProtocol).
  The initial integration discovers `netcoredbg` on `PATH` or through
  `AXAXMLIDE_NETCOREDBG_PATH`; ordinary Run remains independent of it.
- [ ] Connect editor breakpoints to the debug session and show verified,
  unverified, and disabled breakpoint states.
- [ ] Add stepping, continue/pause, stop, and entry-point controls.
- [ ] Add Call Stack, Locals/Variables, Watch, and Debug Console panes.
- [ ] Add exception settings and process attach after launch-and-debug works
  reliably for a selected project.
- [ ] Test debugger behavior against small console and Avalonia sample projects.

The initial integration starts NetCoreDbg for a built .NET assembly, sends open
C# document breakpoints, supports continue/stop, and navigates to the top stack
frame when execution pauses. It is not yet a complete debugger: verified
breakpoint rendering, step controls, locals, watch, launch profiles, and
end-to-end validation across supported platforms remain outstanding. Ordinary
Run remains available without NetCoreDbg.

### 5. Polish and broaden project workflows

- [ ] Add project/solution creation and common Avalonia project templates.
- [ ] Improve build/test feedback, including test discovery and running tests.
- [ ] Add recent workspaces, clearer progress/status, and actionable setup errors.
- [ ] Review accessibility, keyboard navigation, and performance on larger
  solutions.

## Working order

1. Complete the settings/workspace foundations in phase 0.
2. Deliver search and navigation in phase 1, then the code-action/editor loop in
   phase 2.
3. Add editor groups and detachable tabs in phase 3 after document/session state
   is dependable.
4. Start debugger implementation only after launch configuration and breakpoint
   behavior are specified and tested.
5. Use phase 5 to round out project workflows and polish based on real usage.

Each item should be split into a small implementation issue before work starts.
For cross-cutting changes, include acceptance criteria and focused tests with
the issue.

